<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n42868" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">scansĭo</span></b> , ōnis,
			f.
		scando, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a climbing up</i> (very rare). *
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quā simplici scansione scandebant in
					lectum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Varr. L. L. 5, § 168</span></i>
			 Müll.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: sonorum, <i>a
			rising</i> or <i>elevation of tones</i> of the voice,
				<i><span class="en">Vitr. 6,
				1</span></i>. —In gram., <i>a metrical
				reading</i> or <i>scanning</i> of verse, <i>scansion</i>, Beda, p. 2368 P.;
				<i><span class="en">Diom. 494 sq.</span></i> P.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
